<section class="section section--flush">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The constraint I set myself</h2>

      <p>
        I wanted to use htmx, because I like it and because it suits a site this
        size. The obvious problem: the common htmx pattern &mdash; request a
        partial, inject it into a container &mdash; produces pages whose content
        doesn't exist in the initial HTML response. On a site whose purpose is
        demonstrating that I understand crawlability, that would be a
        self-inflicted wound. Anyone evaluating me can press Ctrl+U.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the constraint was: <strong>use htmx without ever producing a URL
        that returns a fragment</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Static-first, enhanced second</h2>

      <p>
        Every page here is a complete, pre-rendered HTML document at a real URL,
        generated at build time by Eleventy. There is no server-side rendering
        at request time and no client-side routing.
      </p>

      <p>
        htmx is layered on top as progressive enhancement using
        <code>hx-boost</code> with <code>hx-select="#main"</code>. When you click
        a link, htmx fetches <em>the same complete document a crawler receives</em>
        and swaps only the <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element. That produces three
        properties I wanted:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No fragment URLs exist.</strong> There is nothing extra to
          crawl, canonicalize, or de-duplicate. The duplicate-content surface is
          zero because there is only ever one representation of each page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It works with JavaScript disabled.</strong> Every link is an
          ordinary anchor with a real <code>href</code>. Turn JS off and the
          site is fully functional, just without the soft navigation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crawlers get everything in the first response.</strong> No
          rendering dependency, no JS execution required for content.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The trade-off is honest: boosted navigation re-downloads the full
        document rather than just the changed region. At this page weight
        that's a non-issue, and I'd rather pay a few kilobytes than introduce a
        crawlability question. On a large content site I'd reconsider.
      </p>

      <h2>The accessibility problem boosted navigation creates</h2>

      <p>
        This is the part most htmx implementations skip. When you swap content
        without a real page load, screen readers are not told anything happened.
        The user hears silence, and focus is left wherever it was &mdash; often
        at the bottom of the previous page.
      </p>

      <p>Three fixes are implemented here:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          A visually hidden <code>aria-live="polite"</code> region announces the
          new page title after each swap.
        </li>
        <li>
          Focus is moved programmatically to <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, which
          carries <code>tabindex="-1"</code> so it can receive focus without
          entering the tab order.
        </li>
        <li>
          The document <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, meta description, canonical
          link, and robots directive are parsed from the fetched response and
          applied to the live document, so the tab, history entry, and any
          JS-executing crawler all see correct metadata.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        There's also a failure path: if a boosted request errors, the script
        falls back to a real browser navigation rather than stranding the user
        on a stale page.
      </p>

      <h2>Performance decisions</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>System font stack</td>
            <td>Zero font requests, zero FOUT, no layout shift from font swap.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Single stylesheet, no framework</td>
            <td>No unused CSS to ship or purge.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AVIF and WebP with JPEG fallback</td>
            <td>The headshot is 15&nbsp;KB as AVIF versus 30&nbsp;KB as JPEG.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Explicit width and height on images</td>
            <td>Reserves layout space, which protects CLS.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>fetchpriority="high"</code> on the hero image</td>
            <td>It is the LCP element on the pages where it appears.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Deferred JavaScript</td>
            <td>Nothing blocks the initial render. Total JS is htmx plus a small enhancement script.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No analytics, no third-party scripts</td>
            <td>Nothing to slow the page, and nothing to disclose about visitors.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>SEO implementation</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Unique title and meta description on every page, generated from front matter so they can't silently drift.</li>
        <li>Self-referencing canonical on every page.</li>
        <li>JSON-LD graph: <code>Person</code>, <code>WebSite</code>, <code>ProfilePage</code> on the about page, <code>Article</code> on case studies, and <code>BreadcrumbList</code> throughout.</li>
        <li>Open Graph and Twitter card metadata with a generated 1200&times;630 image.</li>
        <li><code>sitemap.xml</code> generated from the content collection, so a new page cannot be omitted by forgetting to update it.</li>
        <li>Semantic landmarks, a single <code>h1</code> per page, and a logical heading hierarchy.</li>
        <li>A skip link, visible focus indicators, and WCAG&nbsp;2.2&nbsp;AA contrast.</li>
        <li>A <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> dark variant and <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> handling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>CI, and why it's the interesting part</h2>

      <p>
        Claiming a site is well-optimized is weak evidence. Enforcing it
        mechanically is stronger. Every pull request to this repository runs:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>html-validate</strong> &mdash; markup validity.</li>
        <li><strong>Metadata uniqueness check</strong> &mdash; a script that fails the build if any page is missing a title, description, or canonical, or if any two pages share one.</li>
        <li><strong>lychee</strong> &mdash; internal and external link checking.</li>
        <li><strong>Lighthouse CI</strong> &mdash; with assertions requiring a perfect SEO score and a perfect accessibility score. The build fails if either regresses.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        That last one is the point. The quality claim on this site isn't a claim;
        it's a gate. I can't merge a change that breaks it without the pipeline
        telling me.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting, and an honest trade-off</h2>

      <p>
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages and deployed from <code>main</code>
        by GitHub Actions. Nothing is uploaded by hand; the artifact that passes
        the gates above is the artifact that ships.
      </p>

      <p>
        I originally built this for Cloudflare Workers so I could declare
        security headers &mdash; HSTS, a content security policy,
        <code>X-Content-Type-Options</code> &mdash; in version control. Then I
        checked the DNS and found that attaching a Worker to a custom domain
        requires moving the whole domain's nameservers to Cloudflare. That meant
        migrating a live site's MX and SPF records to host a portfolio. The
        risk was disproportionate to the benefit, so I changed course.
      </p>

      <p>
        GitHub Pages cannot set custom response headers, so that capability is
        genuinely gone. What remains is a <code>referrer</code> meta tag and the
        structural reasons the headers mattered less here than they would
        elsewhere: no forms, no authentication, no cookies, no user input, and
        no third-party scripts. A content security policy mitigates injection;
        this site has no injection surface. HSTS protects credentials in
        transit; there are no credentials.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm documenting the trade-off rather than quietly dropping the claim,
        because deciding which best practice to spend risk on is most of the
        job. If this site handled user input, I'd have made the opposite call.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'd do differently at scale</h2>

      <p>
        For a site this size, the architecture above is close to optimal. If
        this were a thousand-page content site I'd revisit the full-document
        boost pattern, add incremental builds, and introduce a real image CDN
        rather than build-time generation. I mention that because knowing when
        an approach stops scaling is part of knowing the approach.
      </p>
